  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\n- Thunderstorms Possible Late Saturday into Monday. Severe storms \n  possible, but confidence in where/when/if storms impact West \n  Michigan is low at this time.\n\nSurface ridging and poor moisture quality keeps conditions dry over \nthe next day or two, with precipitation chances holding off until \nSaturday Night. Saturday night a weak shortwave crosses the area. \nHowever, poor moisture quality keeps precipitation chances low (20-\n25%) across the area with most seeing a few hundredths of an inch of \nrain or less.\n\nAttention then turns to the Sunday/Monday timeframe where we \ntransition into a Ring of Fire setup on the eastern periphery of an \nupper-level ridge. Long range CAMs and other NWP guidance suggest \nthat one or more clusters form on this ridge and track through Great \nLakes region. Sunday Evening/Night and Monday Evening/Night are the \nfavored windows at this time. However, these setups are well known \nfor being very difficult to nail down the timing and placement of \nany thunderstorms. Some solutions suggest development near peak \nheating Sunday as well, and some suggest the storms miss the region \nall together. This whole timeframe has at least some potential for \nthunderstorms.\n\nForecast soundings show a conditional severe weather threat in this \ntimeframe , meaning that if the storms track through West Michigan, \nthe environment will be favorable for severe weather. Sunday will \nfeature MUCAPE values near or above 2000 J/kg with 40+ knots of deep \nlayer shear, definitely sufficient for severe thunderstorms. The \nsame environment may exist into Monday/Monday Night as well, with a \nmore favorable mid-level jet placement across the area.\n\nBottom line is environment is favorable for one or more rounds of \nsevere thunderstorms early next week. However, confidence in where \nand when those storms would occur (including whether they affect \nWest Michigan) is low given the synoptic pattern and dependence on \nboundaries from previous convection. Will continue to monitor and \nrefine the forecast in the coming days.\n\n- Northwest Flow Continues Into Late Next Week\n\nPost the cold front later Monday into early Tuesday the northwest \nflow pattern continues. Surface ridging returns and low-level \nmoisture diminishes for the mid-week period. This keeps conditons \ndry until Friday when a shortwave may trigger showers.",
